{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The relevant information involves comparing the probabilities of the two lotteries and assessing whether their differences are negligible. Lottery A has a 0.2% probability of a -3000 Euro outcome, while Lottery B has a 0.1% probability of a -6000 Euro outcome. The difference in probabilities is 0.1 percentage points (0.2% - 0.1%), which the reason considers negligible. The maximum (i.e., least negative) outcomes are 0 Euros for both lotteries, but the next-best outcomes are -3000 for A and -6000 for B. Since both lotteries have the same best outcome (0 Euros), the more favorable 'maximum' in a comparative sense would be interpreted as the less severe loss when a loss occurs. However, strictly speaking, the maximum outcome (0 Euros) is identical in both lotteries. Thus, the reason would be indifferent if the maximum outcomes are exactly equal, unless it interprets 'maximum outcome' in a broader sense (e.g., best possible non-zero outcome). Still, the rule can be applied: if probability differences are deemed negligible, the preference hinges on the more favorable maximum outcome. In this case, since the maximum outcomes are equal, the reason might be indifferent, or it could interpret 'more favorable maximum' as the less severe loss, in which case it would prefer A. The applicability is moderate to high, depending on interpretation.",
